MASTER BUILD PROMPT — Excel Toolkit
 Complete Browser-Only Spreadsheet, File Conversion, and Data Utility Platform

1. Mission

Act as a principal software architect, senior React and TypeScript engineer, spreadsheet-engine specialist, document-processing engineer, UI/UX designer, security engineer, and test automation engineer.

Build a complete, production-quality, extensible spreadsheet and data utility SAAS style site named Excel Toolkit with modern UI and animations. No extra AI jargon in the UI.

Repository name: Excel_Toolkit

The platform must provide spreadsheet viewing and editing, file conversion, data cleaning, workbook comparison, formula tools, document utilities, data visualization, security inspection, recovery utilities, and downloadable spreadsheet templates.

Create the actual GitHub-ready project. Implement the code, install compatible dependencies, configure the development environment, run tests, resolve errors, and write documentation.

Do not stop at a plan, prototype, wireframe, directory tree, or collection of placeholder components.

The application must be a static, client-side web application that processes supported files entirely on the user's device.

2. Non-negotiable architecture
 Allowed
 React, TypeScript, and browser-compatible JavaScript.
 Third-party npm packages for parsing, editing, calculation, conversion, compression, OCR, charting, and document generation.
 WebAssembly libraries that execute locally.
 Web Workers for expensive operations.
 Browser-native file, clipboard, canvas, cryptography, and storage APIs.
 IndexedDB and localStorage for explicitly documented browser-local persistence.
 Static hosting, including GitHub Pages.
 Locally bundled workers, WASM binaries, fonts, and processing assets.
 Commercially licensed libraries when their licensing terms are followed.
 Prohibited
 Application backends.
 API routes and server functions.
 Server-side file processing.
 Databases or cloud storage services.
 Remote conversion or formula-calculation services.
 AI models, AI APIs, and hosted AI features.
 Sending user files, workbook contents, formulas, metadata, or generated results to remote services.
 Analytics, advertising, trackers, and session-recording scripts.
 Runtime dependencies on remote processing endpoints.

The rule is simple: third-party libraries are allowed, but supported processing must happen locally in the browser.

Network isolation

Bundle runtime dependencies locally wherever practical.

After the required application assets have loaded, supported operations must continue with the network disabled.

Do not silently download remote workers, OCR models, language packs, or processing code when a user selects a file.

Create automated network-interception tests to detect unexpected external requests and prevent transmission of user data.

Static asset delivery is allowed. File processing must not depend on a remote service.

