14. Implementation phases
 Phase 1: Project foundation

Initialize React, TypeScript, Vite, routing, styling, linting, testing, and shared components.

Phase 2: Design system and navigation

Implement the app shell, homepage, tool directory, categories, search, filters, theme switching, and responsive navigation.

Phase 3: Registry and processing contracts

Implement the typed tool registry, capability declarations, routes, worker contracts, and validation scripts.

Phase 4: File engine

Implement format detection, validation, parsing adapters, workbook types, downloads, worker communication, and error handling.

Phase 5: First functional tools

Implement and test CSV Viewer, CSV Editor, XLSX Viewer, CSV/XLSX converters, JSON/XLSX converters, duplicate removal, blank-row removal, whitespace cleanup, sorting, filtering, find/replace, text-to-columns, column combination, workbook comparison, merge, data profiling, and transpose.

Phase 6: Spreadsheet editor

Implement editing, keyboard navigation, worksheet management, clipboard support, undo/redo, supported formulas, and workbook export.

Phase 7: Formula and visualization tools

Implement formula generators, local formula calculation, charts, grouped summaries, and pivot-style aggregation.

Phase 8: Additional formats and utilities

Implement document conversion, security inspection, metadata tools, templates, and supported repair utilities.

Phase 9: Offline and security validation

Test local processing, network isolation, storage behavior, PWA caching, and resource limits.

Phase 10: Final delivery

Run all tests, fix failures, complete documentation, validate deployment, and report real implementation coverage.

Maintain a working build after every phase.

If the complete suite cannot be finished in one session, record the remaining work and continue with the highest-priority unfinished implementation. Do not replace missing functionality with placeholders.

